WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:09.630
good afternoon, ladies and gentlemen. Welcome aboard.  Captain speaking, we are pretty much

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:13.110
anticipating on-time arrival. In the
scenario where there's a flight

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:17.520
cancellation or some other off-schedule
operation, we've had a system where we

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:21.510
find new seats and flights availability
for those customers. But I think

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:24.570
often times customers didn't necessarily
know that that was the best option for

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:28.410
them. What we wanted to do was create a
system where they could actually see

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:32.669
alternate options. The goal was to
facilitate a better experience for our

00:00:32.669 --> 00:00:36.300
customers on how to get to their
destination in an automated fashion on

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.170
the channel of their choice. So we
partnered with IBM and specifically IBM

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:44.340
Cloud Platform to adopt their Garage
method, pair programming, testing and

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:49.559
development. The original timeline was
actually over a year so American

00:00:49.559 --> 00:00:53.550
approached IBM and asked us if there was any way we could help them shorten that

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:58.109
timeline. From the time that we started
development on dynamic rebooking until

00:00:58.109 --> 00:01:01.079
the time that we actually had customers
using it was about four and a half

00:01:01.079 --> 00:01:05.250
months. We were kind of cautious to
release this product we rolled it out to

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:08.040
a limited number of stations across all
the channels and it really went

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:11.430
flawlessly.
We are now crossing into a zone of

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:14.950
turbulence please keep your seatbelt
fastened.

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:20.170
In our business, hurricanes, storms are
the natural events impact our customers

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:24.100
on fairly regular basis. What happened
and this is true story

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:28.330
the hurricanes hit and nobody said this
is ridiculous our customers were being

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:32.590
impacted by the hurricanes immediately.
Why are we mitigating risk of a

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:36.280
traditional rollout when we have the
power of the clouds and this is exactly

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:41.140
the scenario that our customers need
this capability. We made the decision as

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:45.159
a team that the tool needed to be turned
on and especially in the hardest hit

00:01:45.159 --> 00:01:51.700
areas so we did. Having the confidence to
just flip the switch really came out of

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:56.409
the whole project from the beginning.
This was a unique opportunity for us

00:01:56.409 --> 00:02:07.299
really to embed the business side right
in with the technology team. One of our

00:02:07.299 --> 00:02:11.980
expectations when we were moving to the
IBM Cloud was the scale and the hyper

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:16.750
scale of cloud should relieve our normal
concerns around infrastructure when we

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:21.549
roll out an application globally. So when
the hurricanes hit we put that to a test

00:02:21.549 --> 00:02:25.989
and our confidence was there and the
application worked flawlessly. I think

00:02:25.989 --> 00:02:28.989
people will now realize that these kind
of activities and integration are much

00:02:28.989 --> 00:02:32.410
easier when we can use cloud technology
and especially microservices we can

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:35.680
break problems into much smaller
problems and those get much easier to

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:39.400
wrap your head around and develop. This
is the technology that we need to be

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:45.390
adopting now in order to keep pace with
our customer expectations.

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:48.349
 

